WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.240
How do you find the genes behind a process when nobody knows what they are?

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.540
Before cloning was routine, a researcher could not start from a gene or protein for

3
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.020
something like segmentation, because the products were unknown.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.260
Forward genetics starts from a phenotype in a population carrying random mutations,

5
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.220
and works toward the gene.

6
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.800
A screen makes no guess about which proteins are involved, so it can find mechanisms

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.600
no one had thought to look for.

8
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:41.690
Choose a phenotype you can score, such as belts of spines on a fly larva's cuticle.

9
00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.610
Then break genes at random, in many genomes.

10
00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.270
The chemical EMS alkylates DNA.

11
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:56.390
A jumpstarter P element supplies transposase, which moves a mutator P element to

12
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.070
new places in the fly genome.

13
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:02.043
Breed so that a recessive mutation shows.

14
00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:08.063
In the Heidelberg fly screen, each line carried one mutagenized chromosome.

15
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:14.483
It was bred two more generations, until some embryos had two broken copies of the

16
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:18.843
same gene. Then score every line and keep the mutants.

17
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.907
Then sort the mutants into genes.

18
00:01:23.907 --> 00:01:24.907
Cross two mutants.

19
00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:28.547
Normal offspring usually mean different genes.

20
00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:32.227
A returning defect usually means the same gene.

21
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:38.907
To find the gene, cross the mutant to a polymorphic strain, select mutant F2

22
00:01:38.907 --> 00:01:42.387
recombinants, pooling their descendants and sequencing them.

23
00:01:43.107 --> 00:01:49.527
Its SNPs, single-letter DNA differences, act as markers; their pattern points to

24
00:01:49.527 --> 00:01:50.527
the linked interval.

25
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:53.187
Confirm with complementation or rescue.

26
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:58.887
Here is one real result, from Doitsidou and colleagues in the worm.

27
00:01:59.687 --> 00:02:04.607
Unlinked SNP alleles show up at roughly equal frequency when enough mutant

28
00:02:04.607 --> 00:02:05.967
recombinants are pooled.

29
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:10.007
Linkage to the mutation shows as a departure from that.

30
00:02:10.847 --> 00:02:17.327
Pooling 50 mutant recombinants, at 41-fold coverage, gave a 2.13 megabase

31
00:02:17.327 --> 00:02:23.967
interval. The mutation was in vab-3, the worm ortholog of Pax6 and Eyeless.

32
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.400
Two ways a screen misleads.

33
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.360
First, an assay finds only what it can see.

34
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.280
Some Arabidopsis meiotic defects fell below the detection threshold of a fruit-length

35
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.860
screen. Choose a sensitive readout and state the criterion.

36
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.860
Second, mutagenized strains carry other background mutations.

37
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.700
Backcross, then test the candidate by complementation or rescue.

38
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:59.313
In 1980, Nüsslein-Volhard and Wieschaus found loci that alter the larval segment

39
00:02:59.313 --> 00:03:03.793
pattern. Random breaking was needed because the genes were unknown.

40
00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:07.473
Hedgehog was named from a spiny-larva mutant.

41
00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:16.013
In 1986, Ellis and Horvitz showed that ced-3 and ced-4 are required to start

42
00:03:16.013 --> 00:03:17.773
programmed deaths in the worm.

43
00:03:17.773 --> 00:03:21.333
Cells that would die survive and differentiate.

44
00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:28.243
In 1973, Hartwell and colleagues found temperature-sensitive yeast mutants.

45
00:03:29.203 --> 00:03:33.443
Those alleles let cells grow at low temperature and fail at high.

46
00:03:34.743 --> 00:03:41.103
CDC28 became the handle on Start, the point in G1 where a yeast cell commits to

47
00:03:41.103 --> 00:03:48.683
dividing. In 1980, Novick, Field and Schekman found secretion mutants, most of which

48
00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:52.823
accumulate membrane-enclosed organelles at 37 degrees Celsius.

49
00:03:54.083 --> 00:03:57.183
Conditional mutants were needed because secretion is essential.

50
00:03:58.423 --> 00:04:02.523
Keep this rule. A screen finds only what its assay can see.
